  annotation "note_1771589201727_0", anchor: "proprietary knowledge encoded in her own ice-memory, beyond retrieval or jurisdiction", text: "Place this beside what I wrote in the Hush☽ Twilight entry about the technique's capacity to suppress ice-memory recording itself. If Oeichowei carries the method in her own ice-memory, and the method can silence ice-memory — she possesses a secret that is capable of erasing the record of its own existence. The Nightshelf's jurisdictional headache is the least interesting part of this sentence. The epistemological one kept me staring at the archive wall for longer than I will admit.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771589201727_1", anchor: "The silence between them on this point is, by all accounts, total.", text: "Aishouwa's own entry records that the ☽'micseleia' Dagger has tasted more colony blood than orca. A weapon that chose its wielder, wielded by someone who never questions orders, in service to a leader who never asks the one question that matters. Everyone involved has arrived independently at the conclusion that examining this arrangement would be worse than living inside it. How remarkable that this is, by every measurable outcome, correct.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771589201727_2", anchor: "Three mutually contradictory accounts of the resulting incident were filed without cross-examination", text: "Two contradictory accounts is a disagreement. Three is a project. Someone was ensuring no official version could ever crystallize. Given that Oeichowei positioned the entire sequence from behind a sightline obstruction, the filing irregularity is not a failure of record-keeping. It is the record.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771589201727_3", anchor: "a hesitation before certain terms, as if she is rendering something from a register that does not map neatly onto the Stack's vocabulary", text: "Nightshelf operational terminology. Has to be. Hush☽ Twilight practitioners develop vocabulary for states of suppression that Aurora parlance simply lacks — sixteen gradations of silence, none of which translate cleanly into 'quiet.'", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
